I see the constructor of IdentifiedCommandValidator calls LogTrace on the injected ILogger if Trace level is enabled. To test this, I need to create a test that injects a mock ILogger and verifies that LogTrace is called when IsEnabled(LogLevel.Trace) returns true, and not called otherwise.

I will write an xUnit test using Moq to mock ILogger<IdentifiedCommandValidator> and verify the behavior.

<tool>submit_test(csharp)</tool>
```csharp
using System;
using eShop.Ordering.API.Application.Validations;
using FluentValidation;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;
using Moq;
using Xunit;

namespace eShop.Ordering.API.Application.Validations.Tests
{
    public class IdentifiedCommandValidatorTests
    {
        [Fact]
        public void Constructor_LoggerIsEnabledTrace_LogsTraceMessage()
        {
            // Arrange
            var loggerMock = new Mock<ILogger<IdentifiedCommandValidator>>();
            loggerMock.Setup(l => l.IsEnabled(LogLevel.Trace)).Returns(true);

            // Act
            var validator = new IdentifiedCommandValidator(loggerMock.Object);

            // Assert
            loggerMock.Verify(
                l => l.Log(
                    LogLevel.Trace,
                    It.IsAny<EventId>(),
                    It.Is<It.IsAnyType>((v, t) => v.ToString().Contains("INSTANCE CREATED")),
                    null,
                    It.IsAny<Func<It.IsAnyType, Exception, string>>()),
                Times.Once);
        }

        [Fact]
        public void Constructor_LoggerIsNotEnabledTrace_DoesNotLogTraceMessage()
        {
            // Arrange
            var loggerMock = new Mock<ILogger<IdentifiedCommandValidator>>();
            loggerMock.Setup(l => l.IsEnabled(LogLevel.Trace)).Returns(false);

            // Act
            var validator = new IdentifiedCommandValidator(loggerMock.Object);

            // Assert
            loggerMock.Verify(
                l => l.Log(
                    LogLevel.Trace,
                    It.IsAny<EventId>(),
                    It.IsAny<It.IsAnyType>(),
                    null,
                    It.IsAny<Func<It.IsAnyType, Exception, string>>()),
                Times.Never);
        }
    }
}
```

The tests are not being discovered or run. This usually happens if the test class or methods are not public or if the test project setup is missing. The test class and methods are public, so the issue might be the namespace or the project setup. The namespace looks fine.

